Yes. We can compress everything we’ve uncovered into one coherent model rather than treating “relationships,” “hallucination,” “experience,” “state,” and “LLM output” as separate problems.
I would call the concept:
Relational State Validation
A doctrine for keeping generative systems grounded without requiring control of the model’s internal world.
The central principle is:
The LLM is allowed to generate possibilities. The relational state determines what those possibilities are allowed to become.
That is the foundation.

1. The fundamental separation
The whole system has three different worlds:
A. External Experience
What actually entered the system from experience, observation, events, records, interactions, etc.
This is your grounding layer.
But it is not automatically truth.
An experience can be incomplete, misinterpreted, incorrectly ordered, or simply wrong.

B. LLM Generation
The LLM has its own enormous internal relationship network.
It can generate:
interpretations,
predictions,
explanations,
relationships,
hypotheses,
completions,
imagined connections.
These can be useful or wrong.
The crucial doctrine is:
LLM generation is a candidate, not an established state.
You don’t need to control the LLM’s internal relationships.
You need to control what authority its generated relationships receive.

C. Relational State
This is the controlled external structure.
It contains things like:
entities
 events
 states
 relationships
 temporal ordering
 provenance
 constraints
 dependencies
 observations
 hypotheses
 contradictions
This becomes the system’s external reality model.

2. The central doctrine
Generation does not equal state.
This should probably be the foundational rule of the entire system.
An LLM can say:
A caused B.
That does not automatically create:
A --caused--> B
Instead:
A --caused--> B
becomes a candidate relationship.
The relational system then determines its status.
Possibly:
supported
contradicted
unresolved
hypothesized
superseded
etc.
This single separation prevents an enormous class of problems.

3. Relationships are not just links
A major discovery from our discussion is that a relationship system cannot merely say:
A is connected to B.
The relationship has semantics.
For example:
A --before--> B
is different from:
A --caused--> B
which is different from:
A --similar-to--> B
which is different from:
A --requires--> B
which is different from:
A --part-of--> B
And temporal relationships are especially important.
You may have:
Before state
S₀
↓
Event
E
↓
After state
S₁
The system must not casually project information from S₁ backward into S₀.
That gives us:
State is temporal, not merely relational.

4. The state model
I would make state a first-class object.
Instead of simply storing:
“A relates to B.”
you store something closer to:
At state/time/context X, A had relationship R with B, based on source P, with status S.
Conceptually:
Relationship = (A, R, B, Context, Time, Provenance, Status)
That is much more powerful.
Because now the system can distinguish:
“This relationship existed before the event.”
from:
“This relationship emerged after the event.”
and:
“The LLM inferred this relationship.”
Those are not the same thing.

5. The four epistemic states
I think we should make this extremely simple at the core.
Every meaningful claim/relationship can enter one of four primary states:
SUPPORTED
The available relational evidence supports it.
CONTRADICTED
The available relational structure conflicts with it.
UNKNOWN
The system doesn’t currently have enough information.
HYPOTHESIS
It has been proposed but hasn’t earned sufficient support.
Everything else can be metadata around these four.
This is important because:
Unknown must not become false, and unknown must not become true.
That prevents the system from hallucinating certainty.

6. Hallucination is redefined
Under this doctrine, hallucination isn’t simply:
“The LLM generated something incorrect.”
That’s too broad.
The more useful definition is:
A hallucination becomes dangerous when an unsupported generated candidate is promoted into authoritative state.
That gives us a much cleaner architecture.
The LLM may generate thousands of wrong possibilities internally.
That’s not necessarily catastrophic.
The catastrophe is:
wrong candidate → accepted relationship → authoritative state → future generations treat it as fact
So the system’s job isn’t necessarily to make hallucination impossible.
Its job is to stop unsupported generation from acquiring state authority.

7. This also solves the “both sides can be wrong” problem
We discovered an important issue:
Experience can be wrong.
LLM generation can be wrong.
Therefore:
Experience > LLM
isn’t sufficient.
You shouldn’t simply assign:
experience = truth
 LLM = less trustworthy
Instead:
Experience enters as evidence.
LLM enters as hypothesis.
Relationships between evidence and hypotheses determine state.
For example:
Experience A:
 A happened before B.
LLM hypothesis:
 A caused B.
The system doesn’t say:
“The LLM is wrong.”
It says:
A-before-B = supported
A-caused-B = unresolved
That’s much more precise.

8. The validation doctrine
The generated output should not be matched against the entire relationship system.
That would be computationally and conceptually overwhelming.
Instead:
Claim → Relevant relationships → Local validation → Global consequences
The process is:
1. Generate
LLM produces output.
2. Extract claims
Separate the meaningful assertions from ordinary language.
3. Identify relevant relationships
Find the part of the relational state that actually matters to each claim.
4. Validate
Check:
relationship type,
direction,
temporal position,
known constraints,
provenance,
contradictions,
dependencies.
5. Assign state
Supported / Contradicted / Unknown / Hypothesis.
6. Promote or withhold
Only sufficiently supported claims become authoritative state.

9. Candidate explosion
We also discovered that you cannot deeply validate every generated candidate.
That produces another combinatorial explosion.
So the doctrine needs:
Progressive relational narrowing
Instead of:
10000 candidates → full relationship validation
you do:
10000 candidates
↓
cheap constraints
↓
1000
↓
contextual relevance
↓
100
↓
focused relational validation
↓
10
↓
deep/global validation
↓
accepted candidates
The relationship system becomes the deep validator, not the first filter for every possibility.

10. But don’t simply delete the rejected candidates
Another important point.
A candidate that isn’t currently selected doesn’t necessarily need to disappear.
You can maintain:
Active
Currently being evaluated.
Dormant
Preserved but not consuming significant validation resources.
Rejected
Contradicted strongly enough to discard.
Accepted
Has sufficient support.
This gives the system a living hypothesis frontier.
New information can reactivate dormant candidates.

11. The anti-circularity rule
This is one of the most important doctrines we uncovered.
Never allow:
LLM generation → automatic relationship creation → future LLM generation → validation from that relationship
without provenance separation.
Otherwise you get:
The system eventually validates its own hallucinations.
Therefore every relationship needs provenance.
For example:
Observed
Externally supplied
Derived
Human interpretation
LLM hypothesis
System inference
These must not have identical authority.
And critically:
An LLM-generated relationship cannot silently become an authoritative relationship merely because it exists in the system.

12. Convergence is stronger than source hierarchy
Instead of saying:
“Trust source A more than source B.”
the system can ask:
How many independent relational pathways converge on this claim?
Suppose:
Experience A supports X.
Experience B supports X.
Temporal structure supports X.
Another independent relationship implies X.
Later observations remain consistent with X.
Now X has strong structural convergence.
The LLM’s agreement with X is additional support, not the thing that made X true.
This creates a much more interesting concept:
Relational convergence
A claim becomes stronger as independent structures converge on it.

13. Contradiction becomes a first-class object
A contradiction shouldn’t simply be:
error → delete one side.
Instead:
A-before-B
and
B-before-A
should create a contradiction state.
The contradiction itself becomes something the system can investigate.
Perhaps:
timestamps differ,
entities were confused,
states were conflated,
one source is incorrect,
the relationships refer to different contexts.
This means:
Contradiction is information.
It tells the system where its current state is unstable.

14. State is therefore not a database snapshot
This is another major conceptual step.
Your system isn’t merely:
“Here are the facts.”
It is:
“Here is the current relational state of what is known, inferred, unresolved, contradicted, and changing.”
So state becomes something like:
Current reality model + temporal history + evidence + hypotheses + unresolved regions + contradictions
That makes the system dynamic.

15. The LLM’s role changes completely
Under this model, the LLM isn’t primarily the knowledge authority.
It becomes something more like:
Generative reasoning engine
It can:
propose relationships,
interpret experiences,
generate hypotheses,
search possible explanations,
identify patterns,
predict consequences,
explain states,
suggest missing relationships.
But it cannot simply declare:
“Therefore this is the state.”
The relational system decides that.
So:
LLM = possibility generator
Relational system = state authority
Experience/evidence = grounding inputs
Validation layer = adjudicator

