17. The doctrine in one sentence
I would make this the central statement:
A generative system may propose anything, but nothing becomes part of authoritative state merely because it was generated; it must earn its status through relationships, temporal consistency, provenance, evidence, and surviving contradiction.
That is the doctrine.

18. And the deeper concept
I think the most interesting part of what you’ve been developing is that this isn’t really just a hallucination-prevention system.
It is closer to:
A relational epistemic architecture
It gives an AI system a distinction between:
what was observed
what happened
what is currently believed
what was inferred
what was generated
what remains unknown
what conflicts
what changed
what can be trusted
That is considerably broader than hallucination prevention.
Hallucination becomes one consequence of failing to maintain those distinctions.

19. The doctrine’s core laws
If we want to turn this into something we can actually develop, I would establish these as the 10 laws:
Law 1 — Generation is not state.
Anything generated by the LLM begins as a candidate.
Law 2 — Evidence is not infallibility.
External experience can also be wrong or incomplete.
Law 3 — Relationships carry meaning.
Type, direction, time, context, and dependency matter.
Law 4 — Unknown is a legitimate state.
The system must not manufacture certainty.
Law 5 — Claims are validated locally before globally.
Don’t compare every output against everything.
Law 6 — Candidates are progressively narrowed.
Deep relational validation is reserved for a manageable frontier.
Law 7 — Provenance determines interpretation and authority.
A generated relationship and an observed relationship are not equivalent.
Law 8 — Contradictions are preserved, not silently erased.
Conflict becomes something the system can investigate.
Law 9 — State must be revisable.
New evidence can change old relationships and downstream conclusions.
Law 10 — No self-confirmation.
Generated information must not silently become the evidence that validates future generated information.

20. The shortest possible version
If we compress the entire thing down even further:
Experience provides evidence.
The LLM generates hypotheses.
Relationships provide constraints.
Time provides state.
Provenance provides origin.
Validation determines status.
Contradictions expose instability.
Unknown preserves uncertainty.
Only validated relationships become authoritative state.
That state then becomes the structured context for the next generation cycle.
And I think that is the model we can develop.
It also explains why your original intuition about relationships was important: the relationship system isn’t merely a lookup database attached to an LLM. It becomes the external structure through which generated possibilities are allowed—or not allowed—to become state.
That is the core concept.

